.TH WHY 1 "October 2026" "why 0.5.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
why \- investigate observable process and TCP facts without inventing causes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B why
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-because ]
.RB [ \-\-sample " duration" ]
.I pid
.br
.B why
.B \-\-not
.I tcp:host:port
.RB [ \-\-timeout " duration" ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B why
is an experimental, read-only diagnostic command. It reports observations,
explicit unknowns, and (in process evidence mode) hypotheses that are not
established as causes. It does not claim that a parent process proves intent,
or that a successful TCP connection proves application health.
.PP
Process observations are collected using
.BR ps (1).
Sampling compares two reported snapshots; it does not measure interval CPU
utilization directly. Process identity checks are best effort and cannot
completely exclude PID reuse.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-because
Show the process evidence ledger, hypotheses, and suggested next steps.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print structured JSON instead of human-readable text.
.TP
.BI \-\-sample " duration"
Take two process snapshots separated by the specified duration, from 100ms
to 10s. Durations use Go notation, such as 500ms or 1s.
.TP
.BI \-\-not " tcp:host:port"
Test whether a TCP connection to the specified host and port succeeds.
An IPv6 literal must be bracketed, e.g., tcp:[::1]:5432.
This mode cannot be combined with a PID, \-\-sample, or \-\-because.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
Set the TCP connection timeout (default 2s, maximum 30s).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B why 1
Inspect process 1 and its reported ancestry.
.TP
.B why \-\-because 4821
Display observations and explicitly unproven process hypotheses.
.TP
.B why \-\-sample 1s \-\-json 4821
Compare two snapshots and output JSON.
.TP
.B why \-\-not tcp:localhost:5432
Check whether a TCP handshake succeeds to localhost port 5432.
.TP
.B why \-\-not 'tcp:[::1]:5432' \-\-timeout 500ms
Check the IPv6 loopback address with a shorter timeout.
.SH EXIT STATUS
In
.B \-\-not
mode:
.TP
.B 0
Expected TCP condition is unmet (connection explicitly refused).
.TP
.B 1
Expected TCP condition is met (TCP connection succeeded).
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments or invalid condition.
.TP
.B 3
Insufficient evidence to determine whether the TCP condition is met.
.PP
In process mode, 0 indicates successful reporting, 1 indicates an
inspection error, and 2 indicates invalid arguments.
.SH LIMITATIONS
A TCP handshake only demonstrates connectivity at the time of the attempt.
Timeouts, DNS failures, and other inconclusive errors do not establish a root
cause. The tool does not collect audit history, database query state, or
process creation events. Some process data may be unavailable due to
permissions or platform differences.
.SH FILES
No configuration file is required.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ps (1),
.BR lsof (8),
.BR netstat (1)
.SH AUTHORS
A just-for-fun Unix experiment.
.SH BUGS
Please treat diagnostic conclusions as hypotheses unless supported by
identified evidence. The command is experimental.

.SH EASTER EGG
.B why --why
explains why this command exists. It is a static, lighthearted statement of purpose,
not a system diagnosis. Combine with
.B --json
for machine-readable output.

.SH EASTER EGG
.B why --why
explains the motivation behind the command.
.B why --why --why
(or more repeated
.B --why
flags) reports recursive existential inquiry and recommends going outside.
.B --json
works with either form. This is a joke, not a diagnostic result.
